<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Policarpo Toro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Policarpo Toro was a Chilean naval officer who annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a> (Rapa Nui) for Chile. After visiting the island in 1870 and urging its acquisition, he bought land there from foreign owners and on 9 September 1888 signed an agreement of cession with the ariki <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atamu-tekena">Atamu Tekena</a> and other chiefs. Chile later leased most of the island to a company for sheep ranching.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/policarpo-toro.jpg" alt="Policarpo Toro" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Policarpo_Toro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
